// Exercise difficulty tiers + strength standards (Strength Level, male).
//
// 1) EXERCISE_DIFFICULTY — every library exercise scored on three axes,
//    modelled on how WHOOP's Strength Trainer weights exercises by a
//    "movement profile" (how much of the musculoskeletal system it uses)
//    and RP-style systemic-fatigue rankings:
//      m = muscle mass trained   1 isolation · 2 upper-body compound · 3 lower/full-body compound
//      a = spinal / axial load   0 none · 1 moderate · 2 heavy (bar on back/hands under full load)
//      s = skill / stability     0 machine/cable/supported · 1 free-weight · 2 unilateral/olympic
//    Difficulty points = 2m + a + s  (2–10). Tier: S 9–10 · A 7–8 · B 5–6 · C 3–4 · D 2.
//    The tier sets the effort-score multiplier (0.65 at 2 pts → 1.65 at 10).
//
// 2) STRENGTH_STANDARDS — male 1RM (lb) or max-reps thresholds by bodyweight,
//    scraped from strengthlevel.com on 2026-10-19 (27.9M users, community lifts).
//    Row = [bodyweight, Beginner, Novice, Intermediate, Advanced, Elite].
//    Levels = stronger than 5% / 20% / 50% / 80% / 95% of lifters.
//    unit "lb" → compare the set's Epley e1RM; unit "reps" → compare reps
//    (bodyweight moves; added load converted to equivalent bodyweight reps).
//    EXERCISE_STANDARD_KEY maps app exercise id → Strength Level lift.
//    Exercises with no key (bands, carries w/o data, planks…) get a tier but no rank.

